Title:
BusyWork — Sleep replacement via randomized “real work” to evade behavioral pattern matching

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
BusyWork is a Rust library intended to replace `sleep()` calls with varied, legitimate-looking activity to reduce detection by behavioral pattern matching in EDR, anti-cheat, and dynamic analysis environments. Instead of idling, each invocation randomly selects tasks across multiple categories (compute, memory, filesystem, registry, WinAPI, network, crypto, COM/WMI) and applies jitter to parameters so syscall/API sequences and instruction traces don’t repeat. It emphasizes anti-analysis design: no time-related objects in the binary, per-call task registry rebuild, `black_box` to prevent optimization, and read-only OS interactions to avoid obvious side effects. A key feature is data-flow integration (`.feed()`), which makes the busywork consume real program variables, complicating taint/data-flow analysis and “noise block” isolation. It also chains task outputs through a scratch buffer to create genuine inter-task dependencies. Useful primarily for red teams, malware developers, and cheat developers evaluating or attempting to evade behavioral heuristics.

Technical Focus:
- Behavioral evasion via randomized workload generation (anti-sleep heuristics)
- Windows API/COM/WMI enumeration patterns (read-only)
- Data-flow/taint-analysis resistance using fed variables and scratch-buffer chaining
- Syscall/API trace diversification with parameter jitter and task mixing
- Rust implementation details: feature flags, `black_box`, function-pointer dispatch

Use Cases:
- Replace `Sleep`/idle loops in implants or tooling to avoid “beacon-sleep-beacon” signatures
- Add variable, realistic background activity during sandbox/dynamic analysis
- Generate diverse Windows telemetry (registry/file/WMI/WinAPI/network) to blend with normal app behavior
- Research/testing of EDR behavioral detections and sequence-matching robustness
- Build configurable “noise” profiles via category feature flags (e.g., no network)

Keywords:
Rust, sleep replacement, EDR evasion, behavioral detection, dynamic analysis evasion, syscall sequence randomization, WinAPI, Windows Registry, filesystem enumeration, COM, WMI, WQL, BCrypt, CNG, DNS, HTTP, NTP, jitter, taint analysis, data-flow integration